TI  - Optimizing Delays in Random Access Procedure of DECT-2020 by Estimation of Device States
AU  - Gaidamaka, Yuliya
AU  - Nikolaev, Dmitry
AU  - Gaidamaka, Yuliya
AU  - Koucheryavy, Yevgeni
T2  - 2025 XIX International Symposium on Problems of Redundancy in Information and Control Systems (Redundancy)
AB  - Massive Machine-Type Communication (mMTC) enables the seamless connectivity of billions energy-efficient devices, forming the intelligent infrastructure for smart cities, automated industries, and real-time data-driven decision making. In this paper we investigate the modern mMTC technology Digital Enhanced Cordless Telecommunication New Radio (DECT2020 NR), focusing on random access (RA) procedure. The study develops a mathematical model characterizing the RA phase and proposes a hidden Markov model (HMM)-based method for estimating the number of competing devices. The research introduces and evaluates a delay reduction strategy wherein the RA phase duration is adapted based on the HMM-based estimation of the device state. Numerical results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed approach in optimizing RA performance. The findings contribute to improving DECT-2020 network efficiency by balancing RA and data transmission phases while minimizing latency.
