AU  - Watteyne, Thomas
AB  - Connecting large swarms of low-power micro-robots remains an open challenge: existing communication systems either lack the scalability to connect hundreds of units, or fail to deliver the low latency required for responsive control. For example, while Wi-Fi and BLE are commonly used in swarm robotics, scaling beyond 50 robots remains challenging. We introduce the Micro-robot Access Radio Infrastructure (Mari), a new link layer and architecture that aims to connect thousands of robots. Our TSCH-based link layer runs on commodity BLE radios, providing responsive control of up to 102 nodes using a single gateway. To scale beyond a single gateway, Mari supports multiple non-coordinated gateways with fast node-controlled handovers. We further provide a lightweight infrastructure for real-time remote control and monitoring. We implement Mari on the popular nRF52840 and nRF5340 platforms, and extensively evaluate it in large-scale testbeds. In experiments with 100 nodes connected to a single gateway, Mari achieves a packet delivery ratio above 97%, round-trip latency of 269 ms, and full association time of 3.5 s under a join storm. When moving 100 nodes together between two gateways, we obtain an average handover duration of only 0.34 s. A single node handover time can take less than 50 ms. In a setup with 200 nodes and two gateways, we measure round-trip latency of 274 ms and a packet delivery ratio above 93%. We integrate Mari with the DotBot testbed in an experimental deployment of several hundred robots, enabling remote firmware update of 600 robots in less than 4 min. We contribute Mari as an open-source platform, including firmware, libraries, and performance monitoring tools, enabling researchers to connect swarm systems from a handful of units up to hundreds.
