TI  - Uni-RAT: A Unified DECT-2020 NR and Wi-Fi System for 6G Multi-RAT Networks
AU  - Dang, Yongchao
AU  - Quang, Ngo
AU  - Kalle, Ruttik
AU  - Requena, Jose Costa
AU  - Jäntt, Rikui
AB  - Future 6G networks aim to deliver seamless, secure, and low-latency connectivity across heterogeneous radio access technologies (RATs) such as 5G New Radio (NR), Wi-Fi, and DECT-2020 NR to support emerging applications including extended reality (XR) and the Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT). However, current Multi-RATs systems are operated and managed in isolation, which hinders users and devices from seamlessly leveraging heterogeneous connectivity in a unified and efficient manner. To address this fragmentation, 3GPP has introduced interworking functions such as the N3IWF for untrusted and the TNGF for trusted non-3GPP access, enabling IP-session continuity when users move across heterogeneous networks. Nevertheless, these mechanisms rely on mandatory IPsec, which requires each RAT to maintain a separate secure tunnel to the core network and prevents intra-cell device-to-device (D2D) communication, thereby introducing significant encryption latency, increasing data-plane load, and complicating network management. To overcome these limitations, we propose Uni-RAT, a unified Multi-RATs framework in which DECT-2020 NR operates as the Master-RAT, anchoring 3GPP control-plane security via 5G-AKA and NAS, while Wi-Fi functions as a Secondary-RAT for data-plane data delivery. Uni-RAT employs lightweight Layer-3 WireGuard tunnels to secure both D2D and multi-hop communication, significantly reducing the latency and computational overhead of IPsec-based interworking mechanisms used by N3IWF and TNGF. In addition, a customized SMS-over-NAS signaling mechanism is introduced to distribute WireGuard configuration and keying material, enabling unified authentication and efficient Multi-RATs security management. We implement a full prototype using Nordic nRF9161 DECT-2020 NR devices, Raspberry Pi Wi-Fi nodes, and a Cumucore 5G core, with security-critical functions protected inside Arm TrustZone. Experimental results indicate that Uni-RAT supports Multi-RATs communication with high throughput and low packet loss in intra-cell D2D scenarios, maintains bounded latency and jitter under relay-assisted and inter-cell configurations, and achieves stable UE registration latency below 100 ms with up to 100 concurrent devices under background traffic, demonstrating its practical feasibility and scalability and positioning Uni-RAT as an early architectural blueprint for 6G Multi-RATs networks.
