TI  - Topology-Aware Deep Reinforcement Learning for Multi-Hop URLLC in DECT-2020 NR Mesh Networks
AU  - Rauwolf, Benjamin
AU  - Asif, Awais Bin
AU  - Peissig, Jürgen
T2  - 2026 Joint European Conference on Networks and Communications & 6G Summit (EuCNC/6G Summit)
AB  - DECT-2020 New Radio (NR) is a radio interface technology (RIT) standardized by the European Telecommunications Standards Institute (ETSI) for large-scale massive machine-type communication (mMTC) and Ultra-Reliable and Low-Latency Communications (URLLC) deployments. In DECT2020 NR mesh networks, multi-hop delivery is typically realized by flooding-based forwarding, which is robust but increases network load and does not explicitly optimize end-to-end latency or reliability. In this paper, we propose a topology-aware Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) framework for URLLC-oriented multi-hop routing in DECT-2020 NR mesh networks. Nexthop selection is modeled as a Markov decision process (MDP) whose state combines link-level latency and packet error rate (PER) metrics with graph-structured topology information. An inductive graph neural network (GNN) encoder summarizes the current mesh topology and a per-edge Deep Q-Network (DQN) head selects the next hop using a URLLC-shaped reward that jointly captures end-to-end latency and reliability. To improve scalability, the agent is trained through a curriculum over progressively larger topologies. Simulation results over small to large mesh topologies show that the proposed GNN+DRL routing policy improves the URLLC trade-off compared with both the existing latency-only routing method and the DECT2020 NR flooding procedure. For a representative medium-sized mesh network, it reduces end-to-end latency by up to 19.9 %, lowers end-to-end PER by up to 10 %, and decreases the number of transmissions per delivered packet compared with the existing approach.
